A||||6||2M4 Dale||High speed sputter||||5||
Z||000000||2M4 Dale||08-19-2000||02:42 AM||||A couple of weeks ago my EGR valve broke on my 87 2.5L/5 spd. coupe. (While I was in Louisville at the Fiero Festival) The code pointed to the MAP sensor...wrong. The EGR was broken...SO I replaced it with a EGR from my 84SE 2.5L/4spd. parts car. Now my car is idleing OK but at or near redline I'm getting a pretty hard miss. It didn't do this before. Plugs and wires are new...air cleaner is new (K&N)...oil is fresh. Is the used EGR from the parts car causing this or do I have a new problem ?  ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||08-19-2000||04:08 PM||||could be the used EGR is no good either. might be calibrated diferant but I don't think so.<P>could be a slight vac leak unless you put a new gasket on it.<P>Could be an ignition problem you just didn't notice. DIS motors are really picky about plug gap, wire condition, etc. Also check coil resistance between the towers. (hot and cold) should be between 5000-7000ohms. Even if they measure OK they could be weak or leaking power slightly. Are they the original coils?<P>could be the crank pickup or the PIM as well. The PIM location makes it run really hot. Close to failure temperature at times. It could be the PIM simply is to hot to properly fire at high rpm.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||2M4 Dale||08-20-2000||10:28 AM||||Ogre, the possibility of a vacuum leak is a good one...lol. But the EGR gasket is almost new. I'll check everything. <P>I have a sneaking suspicion that my coils may be the culpret. I believe they are OEM and my ride has 106 thousand on the clock. I'll have to have them checked.<P>Thanks Ogre !!!||||reg||6
Z||000003||theogre||08-20-2000||12:57 PM||||you can get a cheap volt/ohm meter at radio shack and do it yourself.<P>A word of warning..... the DIS brick does run hot because of location. Because of that the PIM doesn't always like aftermarket coils. I have a set of Accell coils and they draw just enough extra current that the PIM overheats and breaks up. Put the OE coils back and it's fine.(I even replaced the PIM to see if it helped. It was better but the new PIM had other problems. Like it fried in about a week.) Other people have had no problem with them. It may depend on exactly what PIM is in the brick.<P>If you take the brick off the car make sure you teflon tape the bolts that go into the block and Replace the O ring on the crank sensor. You will leak oil if you don't do both. (New sensors come with a new O ring.)<P>It's a pain but it is posible to get the coils off and on w/o lifting the whole brick. You need an L shaped peice of metal to hold the heads of the coil bolts into the holes to put the nuts back on. (The coils are held by carriage bolts.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 08-20-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Old Lar||08-20-2000||07:56 PM||||PIM???  What is it?  I need some educating.||||reg||5
Z||000005||theogre||08-20-2000||10:38 PM||||Primary Ignition Module. Most times the word Primary is left out. There is a HOW TO: about dealing with it. It might be in the archives.||||reg||1
Z||000006||2M4 Dale||08-21-2000||12:24 AM||||Thanks Ogre, my father in law is a electrician and he'll loan me a volt meter. When and if I replace anything...I will probably use OEM parts if possible. Just to avoid any "aftermarket" problems.||||reg||14
